## Deployment and Operations

The application is deployed to Amazon EKS using Kubernetes manifests.

Example commands:

```bash
# Configure kubectl for the EKS cluster
aws eks update-kubeconfig \
  --region us-east-1 \
  --name cloud-incident-manager-eks

# Check cluster nodes
kubectl get nodes

# Inspect application resources
kubectl get all -n cloud-incident-manager

# Inspect application logs
kubectl logs deployment/cloud-incident-manager \
  -n cloud-incident-manager

# Inspect Kubernetes events
kubectl get events \
  -n cloud-incident-manager \
  --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp
```

### Image Management

Application and automation images are stored in Amazon ECR.

To inspect published images:

```bash
aws ecr list-images \
  --repository-name cloud-incident-manager \
  --region us-east-1

aws ecr list-images \
  --repository-name cloud-incident-manager-runner \
  --region us-east-1
```

---

## Break-Fix Lab Objectives

The project is intended to evolve into a practical DevOps troubleshooting environment.

The goal is to simulate infrastructure and application failures that an engineer might encounter in a production cloud environment.

Planned troubleshooting scenarios include:

| Area | Example Failure |
|---|---|
| Kubernetes | CrashLoopBackOff |
| Kubernetes | Failed readiness and liveness probes |
| Kubernetes | Incorrect image tags |
| Kubernetes | Missing ConfigMaps or Secrets |
| Networking | Service connectivity failures |
| AWS IAM | Incorrect IAM permissions |
| ECR | Image pull authentication failures |
| Terraform | State and configuration issues |
| Docker | Broken image builds |
| CI/CD | Failed GitHub Actions workflows |
| Python | Application dependency and runtime failures |
| Database | PostgreSQL connectivity failures |

### Automation Runner

A separate Python-based automation runner is included in the repository.

The runner uses Boto3 and is designed to support controlled AWS troubleshooting exercises.

The initial implementation is dry-run oriented. Actual infrastructure-changing functionality is not enabled.

Future development will introduce isolated scenarios, diagnostic output, and guided troubleshooting exercises.

---

## Project Goals

This project demonstrates practical experience with:

- Building and containerizing Python applications.
- Provisioning AWS infrastructure with Terraform.
- Operating workloads on Amazon EKS.
- Building CI pipelines with GitHub Actions.
- Implementing secure GitHub-to-AWS authentication using OIDC.
- Publishing container images to Amazon ECR.
- Troubleshooting Kubernetes workloads and AWS infrastructure.
- Developing Python automation for cloud operations.

The longer-term objective is to create a reusable DevOps break-fix sandbox that simulates real-world infrastructure incidents and provides a practical environment for diagnosing and resolving them.

---

## Security Considerations

- AWS authentication from GitHub Actions uses OIDC rather than static AWS credentials.
- IAM permissions are assigned through dedicated roles and policies.
- Database credentials are supplied through Kubernetes Secrets.
- Terraform state is stored remotely in an encrypted S3 backend.
- Infrastructure-changing automation remains disabled in the initial runner implementation.

This environment is intended for learning and demonstration, not production deployment.

